{"name":"Man Friday","hex":"#3c4c5d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"man","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Mandingo."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An adult male human.","All human males collectively: mankind."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/man"}],"entries":[{"title":"Man Friday (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Man Friday is a 1975 adventure film directed by Jack Gold and starring Peter O'Toole and Richard Roundtree. It is adapted from the 1973 play by Adrian Mitchell based on Daniel Defoe's 1719 novel Robinson Crusoe, but reverses the roles, portraying Crusoe as a blunt, stiff Englishman, while the native he calls Man Friday is much more intelligent and empathic. The film can be regarded as being critical of western civilization, against which it draws a contrasting picture of Caribbean tribal life."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/50/Man_Friday_75.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man_Friday_(film)","wikidata":"Q1454785"},{"title":"Friday (Robinson Crusoe)","blocks":[{"text":"Friday is one of the main characters of Daniel Defoe's 1719 novel Robinson Crusoe and its sequel The Farther Adventures of Robinson Crusoe. Robinson Crusoe names the man Friday, with whom he cannot at first communicate, because they first meet on that day. The character is the source of the expression \"Man Friday\", used to describe a male personal assistant or servant, especially one who is particularly competent or loyal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Robinson_Crusoe_and_Man_Friday_Offterdinger.jpg/330px-Robinson_Crusoe_and_Man_Friday_Offterdinger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friday_(Robinson_Crusoe)","wikidata":"Q3735641"},{"title":"Man","blocks":[{"text":"A man is an adult male human. Before adulthood, a male child or adolescent is referred to as a boy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Outdoors-man-portrait_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Outdoors-man-portrait_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man","wikidata":"Q8441"},{"title":"Friday","blocks":[{"text":"Friday is the day of the week between Thursday and Saturday. In countries adopting the \"Monday-first\" convention it is the fifth day of the week. In countries that adopt the \"Sunday-first\" convention, it is the sixth day of the week."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Friday_Abbr_GW.png/330px-Friday_Abbr_GW.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friday","wikidata":"Q130"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/A._F._Lydon_Robinson_Crusoe_Plate_09_%281865%29.JPG/500px-A._F._Lydon_Robinson_Crusoe_Plate_09_%281865%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/A._F._Lydon_Robinson_Crusoe_Plate_09_%281865%29.JPG/1280px-A._F._Lydon_Robinson_Crusoe_Plate_09_%281865%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/A._F._Lydon_Robinson_Crusoe_Plate_09_%281865%29.JPG/500px-A._F._Lydon_Robinson_Crusoe_Plate_09_%281865%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A. F. Lydon illustration, 1865: \"Robinson Crusoe and Friday attacking the savages\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA._F._Lydon_Robinson_Crusoe_Plate_09_(1865).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Crusoe_Dilke_and_Man_Friday_McKenna_Punch_c1900.png/500px-Crusoe_Dilke_and_Man_Friday_McKenna_Punch_